{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\nResearch how agents can write concise but complete ticket descriptions, plans, and comments. Use current evidence and examples from popular, actively maintained OSS prompt, skill, or Claude-plugin projects.\n\n## Plan\n- Review 2025-2026 primary research on controllable verbosity, compression, and evaluation.\n- Inspect maintained OSS agent-instruction repositories for concrete brevity patterns.\n- Synthesize agent guidance and deterministic bloat signals with false-positive safeguards.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Cite at least three 2025-2026 primary sources and four maintained OSS sources.\n- [x] Distinguish empirical evidence from practitioner convention.\n- [x] Give actionable agent guidance that preserves necessary facts.\n- [x] For each proposed check, state its computable signal, default threshold, severity, and exemption.\n- [x] Identify checks that must remain advisory because they cannot prove irrelevance.\n\n## Verification\nCross-check material claims against cited sources. Confirm the answer explicitly covers (1) agent-writing guidance and (2) deterministic rules for flagging bloated content."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788413546157689001,"uuid":"221970bb-cd1d-4c0a-ad67-a776176b301f"}